'''''Mandevilla'' × ''amabilis''''' is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[family (botany)|family]] [[Apocynaceae]].<ref name="inaturalist-Mandevilla amabilis">{{cite web |title=Mandevilla amabilis |url=https://www.inaturalist.org/taxa/431261-Mandevilla-amabilis |website=iNaturalist |access-date=2022-03-08 |language=en}}</ref> It was described in 1974 in the journal ''[[Baileya (journal)|Baileya]].''<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Lorenzo-Caceres |first=Jose Manuel Sanchez |title=Las especies del género Mandevilla Lindl. (Apocynaceae) cultivadas en Espana |url=https://www.arbolesornamentales.es/Mandevilla.pdf |access-date=2022-03-09 |website=[[arboles ornamentales]]}}</ref> Also known as ''Alice du Pont''.<ref>{{cite web |title=Mandevilla × amabilis 'Alice du Pont' {{!}} mandevilla 'Alice du Pont' Climber Wall Shrub/RHS Gardening |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/120494/mandevilla-x-amabilis-alice-du-pont/details |website=www.rhs.org.uk |access-date=3 April 2022 |language=en-gb}}</ref>

==Description==
Evergreen climbing plant with opposite leaves of thick, rugose texture, with prominent venation. Flowers are pink with a yellow throat.<ref name=":0" />

==Range==
The species has no natural range, having originated in cultivation in the mid-19th century. It is thought to be a hybrid between ''[[Mandevilla splendens]]'' and another, unknown species.<ref>Pacific Island Ecosystems at Risk (PIER){{cite web | url= http://hear.org/pier/wra/pacific/mandevilla_x_amabilis_htmlwra.htm | title = Pacific Island Ecosystems at Risk (PIER) -- Mandevilla x amabilis RISK ASSESSMENT RESULTS}}</ref> Conversely, one source<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Oder |first=Alev |date=2016 |title=A Set of 20 New SSR Markers Developed and Evaluated in Mandevilla Lindl. |journal=Molecules |volume=21 |issue=10 |pages=1316 |doi=10.3390/molecules21101316 |pmid=27706055 |pmc=6274059 |doi-access=free }}</ref> says that it is a natural hybrid occurring in Brazil.

==Taxonomy==
In a molecular analysis, ''M.'' × ''amabilis'' forms a sister clade to a complex that includes the natural species ''[[Mandevilla boliviensis|M. boliviensis]]'' and the Sundaville hybrids.<ref name=":1" />

